  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    Aurora Berg — referred to in most sources simply as **the Berg** — is a supernatural iceberg of a scale that resists conventional measurement. It contains mountains, cavern systems, volcanic veins, and two distinct civilizations, neither of which has mapped its full extent. No penguin has reached its edges and returned to report them. The Berg drifts through an endless frozen sea under perpetual twilight, its substrate composed of ice old enough to have turned blue — ice that predates penguin civilization and, in its deepest strata, predates any species the record can name.
    
    The frozen sea surrounding the Berg is, by every account, infinite. Whether this is literally true or merely operationally so — no expedition having survived long enough to disprove it — remains an open question that the creation traditions answer differently. The Berg may be the only land that exists. It behaves as though it is.
    
    ## The Two Colonies
    
    Two penguin civilizations occupy the Berg in an arrangement that is structural, not elective:
    
    - **Aurora Stack**, the surface colony, clings to the Berg's sunlit face. Its engineered crystal spires catch aurora-light and channel it through galleries where the light refracts through prismatic architectures that serve both liturgical and functional purposes. The spires are load-bearing in more than the physical sense — a collapsed spire severs light-paths that bind the colony's infrastructure to its faith. Governance has fragmented in recent years, with the Icebound Exchange (the dominant merchant guild), the E'frostlings (a territorial faction controlling key trade routes), and splinter groups contesting authority that no single institution now commands.
    
    - **Nightfall Shelf**, the subsurface colony, tunnels into the Berg's body, mining fire-cores from volcanic veins at increasingly unstable depths. Fire-cores — fist-sized crystals of solidified geothermal heat — are the Nightshelf's primary export and the surface colony's primary means of survival. The Midnight Claws, the Wake-Singers (an orca shaman cult operating from the Corpse Current), and various guild factions contest the deep tunnels with a frequency that makes territorial maps unreliable within seasons.
    
    The two colonies are bound by the **Flipper Accord**, a trade agreement exchanging surface aurora-crystals for deep fire-cores. Neither colony can survive without the other's resource. The Accord's public text is the version most scholars cite. The Foundation Depths contain three earlier drafts, each renegotiated in secret, with concessions that the final document does not reflect. The dependency the Accord codifies has cracked at least once: after the Abys∴uina destabilized the volcanic core, surface colonies cut crystal shipments in fear, and the Nightshelf starved in darkness.
    
    ## Three Sources of Power
    
    The Berg harbors three distinct sources of magic:
    
    | Source | Origin | Domain | Primary Users |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | **Aurora-light** | The aurora crowning the Berg | Ice, crystal, divination | Aurora Stack adepts |
    | **Volcanic fire** | Geothermal veins in deep substrate | Forge, transformation, destruction | Nightshelf fire-keepers |
    | **Ice-memory** | Primordial substrate | Blood, force, communion with the deep | Practitioners of both colonies |
    
    Prophetic accounts describe the three fusing at breach-points where the old world has broken open, producing phenomena belonging to none of the three alone. The chronicle of Xuezhaihei Kria'Grace, an aurora observer at the Stack's twenty-third lens, records a vision of precisely such a fusion dissolving the boundary between living and dead. Whether this is prophecy or hallucination is a distinction the sources do not reliably make.
    
    ## Ice-Memory and the Deep Substrate
    
    The Berg's ice records events as literal impressions — frozen scenes, echo-sounds, emotional residue that intensifies with depth. The oldest layers suggest the Berg calved from a body so vast it had no edges: an undifferentiated crystal field that creation scripture calls the **Unbroken Field**. The Nightshelf Recitation attributes the shattering to a single blow from a god beneath the water.
    
    Ice-memory was long considered immutable. It is not. Practitioners of **Snowdrift-numb**, a meditative technique permitting the reading or rewriting of frozen impressions, have altered stored records. The alterations leave layered residue — both the original and the revision persist — and contaminate the thermal signature of anyone who reads the corrupted ice. The chronicle of Zhinghoua lang'Gleam documents a case in which a founding-era leader used Snowdrift-numb to erase the names of families she had allowed to freeze, a corruption I have since confirmed in the relevant Foundation stratum.
    
    Nightshelf elders designate the deepest substrate — where primordial ice meets first stone, before volcanic veins corrupted it — as **memory-ice**, a layer capable of permanently holding objects driven into it along with everything those objects remember. The orca Grul Grulrend is recorded as having entombed the corrupted weapon Grungruk's Claw in this layer, where it remains.
    
    Below the sixth depth, the veil between the living world and the ice-memory grows thinnest. The compressed deep ice harbors presences that predate penguin civilization — patient intelligences that the Nightshelf claim are real and the Aurora claim are made real by belief. The lowest tunnel walls bear symbols written in ash or blood by hands no living species claims. I have seen them. I do not discuss what I felt.
    
    ## Creation Traditions
    
    Three traditions compete to explain the Berg as divine anatomy:
    
    - **Wake-Singer tradition** (orca): The Berg is the corpse of a god killed by an unknown force, split from jaw to skull. The frozen sea is not ocean but absence — the void where no divine body fell.
    - **Aurora liturgy**: The god crystallized itself willingly, its dying radiance carving flesh into habitable form. The vertebrae became ridgeline passages; the open eye became the northern sea.
    - **Nightshelf fire-keepers**: The god is not dead but sleeping. The shapers burrowed from inside a still-warm body whose veins became tunnels and whose clotted blood became fire-cores.
    
    All three traditions agree on one anomaly: the **Omega Obelisk** (designated M7-6HH39N in old kill-tally notation) — seven feet of whale-bone wound at the base with coral, carved with sigils in no readable language — was already embedded in the god's body before the shaping began. Three symbols near its apex were deliberately gouged out in a defacement that predates the Berg itself. The aurora's light passes around the Obelisk as though it belongs to a different world. Recent reports indicate the Obelisk sits higher in its chamber than where it was last placed. The coral at its base, long charred and lifeless, has shown a single pale tendril of new growth.
    
    ## Catastrophes and Structural Decline
    
    The Berg's integrity has deteriorated through a series of catastrophes whose sequence matters:
    
    | Event | Effect |
    |---|---|
    | **The Calving** | Ruptured the eastern face; flooded archives, collapsed galleries, froze colonists in place. Eastern spires no longer catch aurora-light as designed. |
    | **Abyss Collapse Tide** | Split thermal seams beneath the Corpse Current; superheated steam flooded lower passages. Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute, the blood-mage mediating orca negotiations, swam into the fractures and did not return. |
    | **Western shelf collapse** | Orca shore-breakers applied sustained percussive force through eighty body-lengths of stone; tunnels already destabilized by Pta∴extinct aftermath crystallization gave way, destroying the last western supply routes. |
    | **Ongoing deep-vein collapse** | Threatens volcanic mining operations sustaining both colonies' fire-core supply; fire-dependent magical practices grow increasingly unsustainable. |
    
    The spire infrastructure faces a separate threat: resonance transmitted from deep artifacts propagates through the ice along lines the spires were never cut to receive. When the Radiant Aurora-harp activated at Light Ingjeizhi, its frequency passed through foundation crystal into unmapped deep ice, and something in the substrate returned a resonance — completing a circuit broken since before penguins settled the surface.
    
    ## The Presence Below
    
    Something vast moves in the darkness beneath the frozen sea. The orca know. Their raids grow bolder each season. After the Wake-Singers breached deep routes beneath the Fissured Smelt~, the movement shifted from passive to what those in the lowest tunnels describe not as knocking but as *counting*.
    
    The Aurora Stack's mystics refuse to speak of what they see in the light when the aurora goes strange. The Nightshelf's deep-tunnel workers report sustained low-frequency disturbance in the Foundation strata. I have felt it myself. The Council has commissioned this edition in preparation for what they call 'the transition.' I have not been told what the transition is.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771580065110_0", anchor: "concessions that the final document does not reflect", text: "Restrained. The second draft contains a territorial clause ceding deep-route access through what is now ☽'scar Ramtatus~ — access the Aurora Stack had no authority to grant over sovereign Nightshelf territory. The third draft erases this clause and replaces it with a mutual non-acknowledgment provision: both parties agree the clause never existed. The ice disagrees. I have annotated the renegotiations elsewhere and will not rehearse the full sequence here, but the main text's delicacy on this point is a concession of its own.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580065110_1", anchor: "designated M7-6HH39N in old kill-tally notation", text: "Orca cataloguing. Pressure-carved into bone tallies recovered from raiding camps. The notation encodes size, threat-level, and a third-position value that has never been satisfactorily translated — possibly depth, possibly age, possibly a quality for which penguins have no word. That I defaulted to orca designation for the Berg's most sacred object without attribution is the kind of sourcing error I would have bled ink over if anyone else had committed it.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580065110_2", anchor: "All three traditions agree on one anomaly", text: "Stop and hold this. Three cosmologies that cannot agree on whether the god is dead, sleeping, or self-crystallized — traditions whose adherents have killed each other over the distinction — converge without negotiation on a single physical object that predates their disagreement. Convergence in incompatible mythologies is not evidence of shared truth. It is usually evidence of a shared problem. The Obelisk is not explained by any of the three traditions. It is explained *around*. That is not agreement. That is three separate silences shaped like the same wound.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580065110_3", anchor: "has shown a single pale tendril of new growth", text: "The Foundation Depths preserve a stratum from well before the Clever Ice Age in which an observer recorded coral growth at the Obelisk's base in nearly identical terms. I initially assumed source contamination from my own reading. It is not. The current observation comes from a maintenance inspection three seasons ago, independently sourced. Either coral growth at this site is cyclical on a timescale no one has mapped, or the ice is projecting an ancient impression into the physical present. I have catalogued eleven instances of temporal echo at mythic-grade sites. This would be the twelfth, and the most troubling — the maintenance crew did not read ice. They saw it with their eyes.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580065110_4", anchor: "not as knocking but as *counting*", text: "My translation. The deep-tunnel crew's deposition uses the Nightshelf term for sequential percussive contact, which can mean counting but more commonly describes how orca test ice thickness before breaching. \"Counting\" implies intelligence and purpose. \"Testing\" implies hunger. The acoustic signature fits both. I chose the word that kept me awake.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout, source_anchor: "not as knocking but as counting"
  annotation "note_1771580065110_5", anchor: "I have seen them. I do not discuss what I felt.", text: "Putting this in the main text was a lapse. Leaving it there is a decision.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580065110_6", anchor: "the Nightshelf starved in darkness", text: "Three days. The crystal shipment interruption lasted three days before emergency reserves were released. The surface colonies experienced inconvenience. \"Starved in darkness\" is what happened to deep-tunnel crews already on half-rations who were not prioritized for reserves. The entry does not distinguish between the two because the political point is the dependency, not who bears its cost. The cost is never evenly distributed. It never is.", type: :correction, display: :popout
